<%# Student trading, as a setting rather than a switch in the page header.

    It was a bare 36x20 track beside the word "Trading" in the header's action area, next to "Add
    student" and "Edit classroom". Three problems, and they compound:

      - A header action area is for the page's actions. A switch there reads as one of them, so the
        one control on this page that changes what students can do sat among navigation.
      - The state was carried by the track's colour alone. "Trading" plus green is not a sentence; a
        reader had to know that green means on, and nothing said which way the switch was pointing.
      - Nothing said what it *does*. A teacher could not tell from the page whether flipping it stops
        an order in flight, hides prices, or something else.

    Polaris's SettingToggle is the reference: a card that states the setting's current state in words
    and what it means, with the control beside it. Stripe and GitHub both do the same for a
    consequential feature flag - the description carries the consequence, not the control. The switch
    itself is kept rather than replaced with a "Turn off" button: it is the app's existing control,
    its track contrast is measured (green-700 4.79:1 checked, slate-400 border unchecked), and
    trading_toggle_test.rb drives it.

    The date comes from classrooms.trading_disabled_at, which exists so a student's dismissal of the
    "trading is off" callout can expire. Showing it here is free and answers the obvious next
    question. %>
<% on = classroom.trading_enabled? %>

<%# One card, no card header. `_card`'s header is a title behind a hairline, which measured 57px of the
    179px this block used to take - and the title said "Student trading" while the row under it said the
    same thing in a sentence. The sentence is the better of the two, so the header goes.
    Measured: 179px -> 96px, which is what let the roster back above the fold. %>
<section class="tw-card p-5" aria-labelledby="trading-setting-heading">
  <div class="flex flex-wrap items-start justify-between gap-4">
    <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
        <%= render "components/ui/badge",
                   label: on ? "Trading on" : "Trading off",
                   tone: on ? :success : :neutral %>

        <%# The state as a sentence, so it does not rest on the pill's tint or the switch's position,
            and in the student's terms because that is what a teacher is deciding. This is the section's
            accessible name, so the region is announced by what it says rather than by a generic title. %>
        <p id="trading-setting-heading" class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-900">
          <%= on ? "Students can buy and sell shares." : "Students cannot buy or sell." %>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
        <% if on %>
          Turning this off stops new orders. It does not sell anything students already hold.
        <% else %>
          Students still see their portfolio and the trading floor, with a note explaining why.
          Nothing they hold is affected.<% if classroom.trading_disabled_at.present? %>
            Off since <%= l(classroom.trading_disabled_at.to_date, format: :long) %>.<% end %>
        <% end %>
      </p>
    </div>

    <%# The control keeps a visible verb label. A switch with only a state pill beside it leaves the
        reader guessing which of the two the switch reports - Polaris's SettingToggle labels the control
        with the action for the same reason. %>
    <%= form_with url: toggle_trading_classroom_path(classroom), method: :patch, class: "contents" do %>
      <label class="inline-flex shrink-0 cursor-pointer items-center gap-2">
        <span class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-700 select-none">
          <%= on ? "Turn off" : "Turn on" %>
        </span>
        <%= check_box_tag "trading", "1", on,
                          class: "peer sr-only",
                          disabled: !policy(classroom).toggle_trading?,
                          data: { controller: "submit-on-change",
                                  action: "change->submit-on-change#submit" } %>
        <%# The track needs 3:1 against the page as a UI boundary: green-700 is 4.79:1 checked,
            slate-400 is 2.9:1 unchecked, so the unchecked track also carries a border. %>
        <div class="peer relative h-5 w-9 rounded-full border border-slate-400 bg-slate-300 after:absolute after:top-0.5 after:start-0.5 after:h-4 after:w-4 after:rounded-full after:bg-white after:transition-all after:content-[''] peer-checked:border-green-700 peer-checked:bg-green-700 peer-checked:after:translate-x-full rtl:peer-checked:after:-translate-x-full"></div>
      </label>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>
